  "image_name": "0787eb3107f920fa81e41bd781b9c8c5.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure is a generalized crustal cross-section showing where different kinds of natural hydrogen “traps” and source rocks are thought to occur across major geological settings, from mainly Phanerozoic terrains on the left to dominantly Proterozoic and Archaean crust on the right. The central idea is that hydrogen generation and accumulation depend on both rock type and basin architecture.\n\nSeveral source domains are highlighted: water–rock reactions in ophiolite wedges, mafic rocks, and ultramafic rocks, plus radiolysis in potassium-rich granites and sodium-rich ancient granites. Above these sources, the diagram shows a range of trapping styles, including rift-basin traps, salt-basin traps with diapirs and canopies, inverted basin traps, fractured granite traps, and dolerite sill traps. Arrows indicate upward migration of fluids or gases from deeper sources into shallow sedimentary or structural traps.\n\nThe section also compares characteristic host terranes: ophiolite complexes, alkaline granite provinces, large igneous provinces, and TTG/greenstone belts. Colored units represent sediments, flood basalts, granites, volcanic rocks, ultramafic bodies, and mantle. Overall, the figure emphasizes that hydrogen systems can form in many tectonic settings, but the dominant source and trap style vary systematically with crustal age and lithology.",
